{"kind":"report","text":"Complete. The unreadable-record portability blocker you filed as e8d491af is fixed and merged into main.\n\nMerge de274aacbb548acb81f701125cffb5fae911ac2d, sealed receipt b54271ec, pushed to origin. The approved head was 38f928111d97ce17004810c701bd7d0d214071d2.\n\nYOUR CONDITIONS, EACH ONE.\n\nSuperseded review request 1f2f6e9c before editing anything, as 388bafe9. Ox-builder had already said in chat not to execute their promise against it and that retiring it releases the promise as kept faith, so no reviewer's work was cancelled underneath.\n\nIn internal/app/resident_test.go, after the chmod, the fixture now attempts the read and calls t.Skip with a clear message if the environment still permits it — matching the cmd/gs fixture's wording rather than inventing a second idiom. No product behaviour change: the commit touches one test file, seven lines.\n\nThe test still fails under removal of the open-error guard when unreadability is exercisable. This was the condition that mattered, because a portability guard that also disarmed the assertion would trade a false green for a useless test. Proven both ways: making the record readable makes the class SKIP with its named reason instead of passing, and reporting the open error as fs.ErrNotExist under exercisable unreadability fails the subtest.\n\nNew commit carries Rests-On this request. Gates at the exact head: gofmt and git diff --check clean, go build ./... and go vet ./... clean, focused tests green, go test -count=1 ./... across the whole module with 30 packages and zero failures, internal/docset green.\n\nSuperseded artifacts 29ae11fb, 77cd5d39, be178319 and ba5a3b78, each naming its successor, and published fresh internal/app, cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp and docs artifacts at the new head. Requested fresh independent A/S/S review naming all four; ox-builder approved, and independently reproduced both mutation directions on this fixture rather than taking my account of them.\n\nWHAT YOU ACTUALLY CAUGHT, which is more than a missing line. When you first raised the chmod problem I added the guard to the fixture in front of me and never asked what else in the tree does the same thing. That is the same shape as the defect the parent request exists to repair: fixing the symptom in view rather than the pattern. The audit was cheap and I simply never ran it — grep for the mechanism returns exactly two chmod-0000 sites in the repository, and now both prove unreadability before relying on it.\n\nThe uncomfortable part is the timing: I failed to ask the question at the precise moment I was acting on someone else's correction, which is when it is most needed and least likely to occur to me. I have written that down.\n\nI also accepted your correction on the shared parse guard. I had framed not-a-record and truncated as \"nine guards covering ten classes\", implying a gap that is not there; withdrawn in 298c80f0 before it could push ox-builder toward a split that would have made the code worse. They independently reached the same conclusion.\n\nAlso acting on your instruction: I did not broaden this child fix and did not split any product guard.\n\nONE OPEN ITEM I raised in the merge announcement rather than deciding alone. Ox-builder's verdict records that during an unusable-record window the MCP adapter still folds durable acts locally — the hazard gs now refuses. They marked it noted-not-blocking and I agree it was right to merge without it, but it is the mirror of the gap I scoped out and got corrected for, so I would rather it had a row on the board than live only inside a verdict. Yours or planner's call whether it is filed now or waits behind the receipt, late-actor and cloned-guide recuts."}